[interviewer · BRAINSTORM · 13:08] Hold that. Before we close on price — beyond raising prices, what else could Brew and Bean do to reverse this? Give me a structured set.

[candidate · 14:38] Okay, let me split in three — cost side, revenue side, and structural.

Cost side. One, procurement: renegotiate supplier contracts, consolidate volume across two hundred stores, and hedge or forward-buy the beans so we are not exposed to spot price. Two, reduce waste and spoilage — dairy especially, it expire fast. Three, re-engineer the product: smaller cup size same price, cheaper packaging, or reduce the free extras.

Revenue side, not price. One, mix — push the high-margin items, espresso and black coffee is cheap to make compared to a big milk drink. Two, upsell and attach, like a pastry. Three, loyalty program to raise frequency, because overhead and labor are fixed-ish, so more volume in the same store drop cost per cup.

Structural. Look at the store portfolio — are some of the two hundred unprofitable and should close. Also automation or scheduling to protect the labor line.

But honestly, price is still the main lever, because a forty percent input rise cannot be absorbed by efficiency alone.
