[interviewer · BRAINSTORM · 14:45] Understood. Of those levers, which would you sequence first, and why?

[candidate silent 20s]

[candidate · 16:35] I sequence by speed and by risk.

First, price — but not blanket. I do a targeted increase, maybe eight to ten percent, on the items where customer is least price-sensitive, espresso drinks, and I test in a few markets before roll to two hundred. This is fastest, and the math show it is the only lever big enough. Ten percent get us to roughly fourteen and half from six.

Second, in parallel, procurement. Renegotiate and hedge. This take three to six months, no customer risk at all, so there is no reason to wait.

Third, mix and upsell. Slower, need training and menu redesign, but it protect us if the price rise cost us some volume.

Last, store closures. That is irreversible and we do not have store-level data yet, so I would not touch it now.

The logic is: fast and reversible first, slow and permanent last.
