[candidate · 18:28] That data actually change one thing, so let me fold it in.

Ticket is up ten percent but menu price flat, so customers buy more items per visit. Combine with revenue plus fifteen — most of the growth is from attach, not price, not new customer. And the extra items are likely food and milk drinks, which are the same inputs that inflate thirty-seven point five percent. So we are growing the low-margin part of the basket. That is bad growth.

My recommendation: Brew & Bean must raise menu prices around ten percent, targeted, immediately.

Why. Input costs rose forty percent, price rose zero. That gap alone is sixteen of the eighteen margin points — beans four, other inputs twelve. Labor flat, overhead only two. Ten percent price take us from six to about fourteen and a half.

Risk: volume drop. So test in a few markets, and go first on espresso where sensitivity is lower.

Next steps: pilot pricing, renegotiate and hedge supply, and get item-level margin to fix the mix.
